+# Agent Note: Account-scoped shared `/api` transport
+
+Status: implemented
+
+English | [中文](2026-09-25-shared-api-account-scope.zh.md)
+
+## Problem
+
+The Harness Web transport predates the PhysicsOS account layer. Its
+`trustedHosts` fence distinguishes authorities, not people: after a deployment
+is reachable beyond loopback, any caller can list sessions, resume another
+account's workspace, prompt an agent with host tools, or read a session export.
+The browser already authenticated `/physicsos/*`, but the generic `/api`
+surface bypassed that identity entirely.
+
+Authentication alone is insufficient. Sessions and workspaces are durable
+resources without an owner field, and the process-level event streams publish
+frames for every account. A login gate that leaves global list, history, and
+WebSocket behavior intact still leaks one account's work to another.
+
+## Decision
+
+`client-connection` exposes an optional `apiPolicy` seam around the complete
+shared `/api` dispatcher. The wrapper runs before both the Typert Gateway
+interceptor and the legacy API Proxy fallback, so a newly registered method
+cannot bypass the account boundary. WebSocket upgrades resolve a scoped event
+source through the same policy before `WebSocketDownlinks` opens a stream.
+
+`auth-host` provides the policy. It resolves the existing
+`physicsos_session` cookie through `AuthService`, stores resource ownership in
+the new `api_resources` table, and applies these rules to non-administrators:
+
+- every request needs a live account session;
+- listed sessions, searches, workspace rows, archive state, and event frames
+  are filtered to owned resources;
+- session, subagent, goal, skill, and nested gateway payloads naming an
+  unowned `sessionId` are indistinguishable from missing sessions;
+- workspace mutations require an owned `workspaceId`;
+- `session.create` ignores caller `cwd`, arbitrary workspace selection, and
+  preallocated session ids; it uses the account's private workspace and the
+  `physics-student` preset;
+- `agentPreset.select` refuses every preset except `physics-student`, while
+  preset authoring and host path/configuration methods remain administrator-only;
+- `/api/respond` is admitted only after the same account received the matching
+  approval/question `rpcId` on its scoped mux stream; that pending map is
+  process-local with a 15-minute TTL.
+
+The private workspace is deterministic per account: a SHA-256 prefix of the
+user key under `workspaceRoot` (default
+`dshHomePath('physicsos-users')`). Its path is created host-side, registered
+once in the workspace registry, and reported by `host.describe` instead of the
+deployment root.
+
+`SUPER_ADMIN` keeps the unscoped troubleshooting view. Resources without an
+ownership row remain administrator-only; they are never guessed into an
+account.
+
+## Alternatives considered
+
+**Keep `/api` loopback-only and move the browser to a private BFF.** This
+would harden the transport but require reimplementing sessions, history,
+models, prompts, approvals, queue control, and two event streams as a second
+protocol. The policy seam keeps the shipped browser client and narrows the
+change to one dispatch boundary.
+
+**Start one Harness process or storage root per account.** Strong isolation,
+but it multiplies model/runtime state, makes shared school data difficult, and
+does not solve the already-running process-level event fan-out without another
+router.
+
+**Infer ownership from session-id prefixes.** Session forks, subagents, and
+workspace ids are generated by the host, so a prefix would require rewriting
+durable identifiers after creation. A persisted ownership row keeps the
+existing id formats and survives restart.
+
+**Filter only in the browser client.** A browser cannot protect a shared
+server; direct HTTP and WebSocket calls would still see every resource.
+
+## Consequences
+
+The generic API now fails closed for unowned legacy resources, so an existing
+single-user installation needs an explicit ownership migration or administrator
+management before those sessions appear in an ordinary account. The policy
+also adds one JSON clone/parse and list/frame filtering per remote call; local
+compositions that do not mount `apiPolicy` keep the original path unchanged.
+
+Pending response ownership is intentionally process-local. A clustered
+deployment must replace that TTL map with a shared one-time ledger even though
+the session/workspace ownership rows themselves are durable.
+
+## Testing
+
+`auth-host/tests/api-policy.spec.ts` pins anonymous refusal, list/direct/nested
+ownership, private workspace and preset rewriting, event filtering, and
+one-time `/api/respond` correlation. `api-scope.spec.ts` pins durable ownership
+and cross-account claim refusal. The Loader composition test boots WebServer,
+storage, auth-host, a real Connection route, and a fake API Proxy; it verifies
+401 before login, owned session creation/listing, and an empty list for a
+second account. Connection tests pin both the policy wrapper around claimed and
+fallback endpoints and the per-upgrade event source.
